we're letting the City Council know that  they can't just come in and Trump the  will of the people and that when they do  things that are very unpopular the  people can stand up and say stop I'm  Tracy Oppenheimer for reason TV i'm here  with Don Duncan California director of  Americans for safe access which has done  a lot of work with medical marijuana  nationwide and particularly in Southern  California Don thanks for being here  thank you very much for having me  earlier this summer LA City Council  passed a ban on medical marijuana  facilities can you talk a little bit  about why the ban was enacted in Los  Angeles we've had a very controversial  experience with medical cannabis  beginning in about 2005 of cooperatives  and collectives started to open here in  the city of Los Angeles for the first  time and the City Council was very slow  to react to that and so the number of  facilities in Los Angeles really grew  very quickly so in 2010 we finally  succeeded in adopting an ordinance in  Los Angeles to help regulate these  facilities unfortunately that was  followed by numerous lawsuits filed by  collectives and cooperatives and by the  City Attorney's Office and so after  years of frustration almost two years in  court the City Council essentially throw  up its hands and walked away from the  issue and said we're just going to ban  these organizations and Councilman Jose  Huizar offered the man he was saying you  know it's gotten excessive because  there's a thousand dispensaries and be  too in some neighborhoods and enough is  enough do you think this was  justification for the band while there  are a few that are operated badly or  located in a questionable location that  doesn't really justify throwing out the  baby with the bathwater people in Los  Angeles want medical cannabis  seventy-seven percent of Californians  support the regulation and the control  of medical cannabis a lot of opponents  also say that it's these dispensaries  are just a farce for stoners to get  legal weed and aren't really for  medicinal purposes there are probably  people out there abusing the system but  again it doesn't justify victimizing the  majority of people who are getting real  medical benefit just like the voters  intended people will find an anecdote  one situation where there was a burglary  or a robbery or someone behaving in a  fraudulent manner and they'll say that's  proof it's all all fraudulent we have to  throw the whole thing out and that just  doesn't stand up to any scrutiny like  you said the support for medical  cannabis is really  strong and residents came out with this  petition can you talk a little bit about  the petition and have the referendum was  successful well after the City Council  adopted the ban patience and collective  operators and organized labor all got  together and organized a referendum  campaign we collected 50,000 signatures  in a period of about nine or ten days  which is a really impressive number we  were able to submit those early to the  City Clerk's office and actually block  the implementation of the band before it  even took effect and so that's a  tremendous victory for patients the next  step is for that to go to the voters in  the city of Los Angeles remand ask them  to overturn the ban adopted by the City  Council otherwise they can use the voter  initiative process which is a tool  voters in california also have where  they can create their own ordinance that  may actually and ironically be a  something city council's favor because  it takes the political pressure off of  them nobody can criticize known for  saying oh you did this you wrote this  bad ordinance the voters sort of take  that authority and it's a shame that has  to come into play in our system but I  think very often politicians prefer the  safest route and putting it off on the  people may be the safest route let's  talk about the future after this band  was blocked or at least for now it shows  very strong support for medical  marijuana and also a check on local  government power for now do you think  this will hold or they'll bring in the  feds like other cities and i think the  referendum changes the situation in los  angeles because it really put very  clearly in the public view that people  don't support the ban and so they want  something else so the city council of  course has a choice now to either just  hold off for the election and then deal  with the consequences in march or they  could take this these few months but  leading up to march to do something good  for the city of Los Angeles they could  adopt a new ordinance they could go back  to the table and sit down with advocates  and with patients and try to work it out  and we really would like for them to do  that but if they don't then of course  we're prepared to go to the ballot we're  prepared to repeal the ban and then move  forward we certainly hope that the city  would be reasonable and not bring in  federal authorities or start prosecuting  people indiscriminately it doesn't look  like that's the path they're going down  we've seen a couple of motions passed  the City Council calling for federal  cooperation when it comes to medical  cannabis we've also seen a call for the  police department to coordinate with a  district attorney's office which is of  course a precursor to bringing felony  charges don't they  want to value of the supporters one  because that secures their own  re-election well we would expect of  course that the City Council members  would do what's most popular but  unfortunately I think that very often  what they hear from another community  are people who are dissatisfied and not  people who are satisfied it's the the  neighbor with one shop on each side of  their house or the the community where  they've had a burglary or a robbery or  something like that those are the people  who are speaking up and really drawing a  lot of attention and sort of driving the  agenda right now other complaints that  it was it was supposed to be enacted to  protect collectives and people growing  their own marijuana and it doesn't  include people making money off of it do  you think there's a problem with the  profits that come along with  dispensaries in our consumer society all  the things we need are provided through  the market economy so if you needed  insulin because you're diabetic you  would buy that at a store and it would  be made by a corporation that  manufactures and packages and ships this  to the pharmacy now California law is a  little bit ambivalent on the terms in  terms of profit regarding medical  cannabis SB 420 which was adopted by the  state legislature in 2003 says that the  nothing in that statute authorizes the  cultivation of cannabis for profit  what's really important about that  nonprofit designation is that we have to  remember that just because an  organization is nonprofit doesn't mean  that no money is going to change hands  when patients visit their collective or  cooperative they reimburse that  collective or cooperative using cash or  credit card for the medicine that they  receive and we have to be very careful  about how our opponents spend that issue  because there's no practical way to get  patients medicine through their  collectives and cooperatives without  having money involved in the transaction  so do you think the the referendum  putting this on hold is it is it more  promising than it is negative I think  whether the referendum is a good thing  or a bad thing depends on which side of  this debate your honor but I think on  the whole this is a very good thing  because we're letting the City Council  know that they can't just come in and  Trump the will of the people there may  yet be one more hurdle for medical  cannabis in Los Angeles in 2010 the  ordinance that the City Council adopted  included what we call a sunset clause  which says that if the city council  doesn't renew that ordinance within two  years it would automatically expire  and every collective and cooperative in  Los Angeles should close and so the city  attorney argues now that collectives and  cooperatives are illegal no matter what  happens with the referendum in Los  Angeles our argument is that it's  unreasonable every single milestone of  implementation was missed registration  deadlines a selection of applicants the  vetting process all of that was missed  along the way the city attorney is  committed to the fact that medical  cannabis is illegal and so we expect  some sort of an enforcement action from  the city attorney that will probably  involve civil measures but that's also  almost certainly going to be coupled  with some law enforcement activity the  LAPD perhaps in cooperation with federal  officials going in and shutting down  facilities and confiscating medicine and  unfortunately that means that some  people who are trying to do right and  are trying to take care of sick people  and obey the law or getting a caught up  in that dragnet yeah well thanks for  coming in okay great thank you for  having me friesen TV I'm Tracy  Oppenheimer

